{# Bundle base: the SHELL's page frame plus the sheets a patrol screen needs.

   Every patrol screen extends this rather than the shell directly, so the
   module's vocabulary loads only where the module renders and the file-preview
   overlay is linked exactly once per page.

   THE FRAME IS SHELLBUNDLE'S, not this module's. Before
   the fleet had a shell, these pages extended a `layout.html.twig` an
   application was expected to provide, and typed their own `<div class="page">`
   and their own flash loop inside it — which is why both are gone from here:
   the trail, the page head, the area's tab strip and the flashes are the
   frame's sockets now, and a module that types its own copies is a module that
   drifts from every other screen in the installation.

   THE TAB STRIP IS LEFT AT ITS DEFAULT, deliberately, and it is the one line
   worth reading twice. Patrols is an AREA-SCOPED module — every route under
   this base sits at /areas/{uuid}/modules/patrols — so the area's own strip is
   exactly right above it, and blanking the socket (which an org-wide hub like
   the files hub does) would be this module claiming to be nowhere in
   particular.

   THE WIDGET MODULE'S SHEET IS NOT OPTIONAL. The dashboard IS a widget
   dashboard, so `.w-grid`, `.w-cell`, `.w-span-*` and the whole library chrome
   are drawn by the shell's widget stylesheet, which its own header says
   must be linked by the page rendering it. Without it the grid has nothing
   declaring a span and every widget draws one column wide.

   Ordered map → widget → preview → patrol, weakest to strongest: this
   module's own sheet is the one allowed to override, and it is last.

   Symfony docs — https://symfony.com/doc/current/frontend/asset_mapper.html :
   "All bundles that have a Resources/public/ or public/ directory will
   automatically have that directory added as an asset path, using the namespace:
   bundles/<BundleName>" — and under AssetMapper those assets are automatically
   content-versioned. So public/patrol.css is served as
   bundles/uhifadhipatrol/patrol.css with a digest, no assets:install. #}
{% extends '@Shell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {# THE MAP STYLESHEET — the atlas's ONE map.css: the plate column, the imagery
       frame, the zoom pills, the base-layer menu, the live scale bar, the
       scroll-bargain hint, the legend and the fullscreen layout that every plate
       in the product wears. render_map() emits the markup those rules dress, so
       without this sheet a patrol map is an unstyled box. Leaflet's own sheet is
       not linked beside it: the UX Map Leaflet bridge's controller imports it,
       so there is one Leaflet and one copy of its styles. Loaded BEFORE
       patrol.css so the module's own rules still win where they must. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Bundle\\AtlasBundle\\AtlasBundle::STYLESHEET')) }}">
    {# The dashboard grid's own vocabulary, from the bundle that owns it. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Bundle\\ShellBundle\\ShellBundle::WIDGET_STYLESHEET')) }}">
    {# The FILE PREVIEW's vocabulary, from the bundle that owns it. A patrol
       photograph opens in the same overlay a file on the hub opens in, so this
       module draws none of it and states none of it — it loads the component's
       sheet and includes the component's partial.

       Unconditional, like the evidence route the photos card already asks for:
       this module refuses to boot without uhifadhi/storage-module (see
       UhifadhiPatrolBundle), because that is where its photographs live.
       → @UhifadhiStorage/overlay/_preview.html.twig #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('bundles/uhifadhistorage/preview.css') }}">
    {# The path is the bundle's own constant, not a literal: the area overview is
       rendered by the AREA module, which links this same sheet from the
       contributor's stylesheet() — and a path typed twice is a path that
       eventually differs. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Patrol\\UhifadhiPatrolBundle::STYLESHEET')) }}">
{% endblock %}

{# THE TRAIL'S SHARED PREFIX, said once. Every patrol screen sits under the same
   four steps — the installation, the areas, this area, its modules — and only
   the tail differs, so screens fill `patrol_trail_tail` and never retype the
   prefix. Each step is a link only where the installation mounted the screen it
   names; see PatrolTrailExtension. #}
{% block shell_breadcrumbs %}
    {%- set areas = patrol_url('area_index') -%}
    {%- set here = patrol_url('area_show', {uuid: area.uuid}) -%}
    {%- set modules = patrol_url('area_modules', {uuid: area.uuid}) -%}
    {%- if areas %}<a href="{{ areas }}">areas</a>{% else %}areas{% endif %} /
    {%- if here %} <a href="{{ here }}">{{ area.name|lower }}</a>{% else %} {{ area.name|lower }}{% endif %} /
    {%- if modules %} <a href="{{ modules }}">modules</a>{% else %} modules{% endif %} /
    {%- block patrol_trail_tail %} patrols{% endblock %}
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page %}{% block patrol_page %}{% endblock %}{% endblock %}
